The Mac SDK Chromium names as its minimum, on the build host.

build/config/mac/mac_sdk_overrides.gni says mac_sdk_min = "15", and
base/process/launch_mac.cc calls posix_spawn_file_actions_addchdir - the
POSIX 2024 spelling, which only the macOS 26 SDK declares - behind
__builtin_available(macOS 26, *). __builtin_available decides at run time
whether the call is MADE; it cannot make an undeclared function compile, so
with any SDK older than 26 the host half of every build stops in //base.

Chromium's own bots build with 26.5 and never see it. An Intel Mac on
Sonoma cannot: Xcode 16.2 is the newest that runs there and its SDK is 15.2,
which meets the stated minimum. The declaration is asked of the SDK with the
SDK's own macro, so a 26 SDK compiles exactly what it compiled before and an
older one compiles the _np call that was always the fallback.

This is the build host's patch, not the target's: launch_mac.cc is compiled
only for the mac toolchain.
diff --git a/base/process/launch_mac.cc b/base/process/launch_mac.cc
--- a/base/process/launch_mac.cc
+++ b/base/process/launch_mac.cc
@@ -160,11 +160,13 @@
 
 #if BUILDFLAG(IS_MAC)
   void Chdir(const char* path) {
+#if defined(MAC_OS_VERSION_26_0)
     if (__builtin_available(macOS 26, *)) {
       DPSXCHECK(posix_spawn_file_actions_addchdir(&file_actions_, path));
-    } else {
-      DPSXCHECK(posix_spawn_file_actions_addchdir_np(&file_actions_, path));
+      return;
     }
+#endif
+    DPSXCHECK(posix_spawn_file_actions_addchdir_np(&file_actions_, path));
   }
 #endif
 
